# Before you start

The objective of this tutorial is to show how to place an interactive 3D model, written in C++ with OpenGL,
inside the slides of a presentation. The tutorial is organized as a series of complete examples, from the
simplest (a rotating cube in raw OpenGL) to more advanced ones (marching cubes with an ImGui window, points
dragged with the mouse). Each example is a folder that you can download, build, and show in a slide as it
is, and each page displays all its files.

*(You can start with the first example, then go as far as you need: the examples 3 to 6 use the CGP library,
the two first ones only need GLFW.)*

## Principle

A slide displays interactive 3D in the same way as a browser does: as a web page, inside a frame (`<iframe>`).
Three elements are involved:

<div class="outputs">
<div class="output"><div class="output-name">1. Your program, for the web</div><p>Your C++ OpenGL program, compiled to WebAssembly by Emscripten: <code>project.js</code>, <code>project.wasm</code>.</p></div>
<div class="output"><div class="output-name">2. The page of the demo</div><p><code>slide-frame.html</code>: it runs the program in the frame, and passes it the settings given in its address.</p></div>
<div class="output"><div class="output-name">3. The slide</div><p>A frame displays this page; buttons in the slide can drive the program, and the arrow keys still go to the next slide.</p></div>
</div>

- The slides of the examples are made with [reveal.js](https://revealjs.com/) (each example comes with a
  slide, `slides.html`).
- The same demos are also used in a talk made with [lhtml-site](https://scholartools.fr/lhtml/).
- Both tools generate HTML pages, where a frame is a native element.

## Requirements

- **A C++ compiler and CMake**, as well as GLFW for the desktop version of the examples
  (`brew install glfw` on macOS, `sudo apt install libglfw3-dev` on Ubuntu).
- **The Emscripten SDK**, to compile the web version:

  ```
  git clone https://github.com/emscripten-core/emsdk.git
  cd emsdk
  ./emsdk install latest
  ./emsdk activate latest
  source ./emsdk_env.sh        # in each new terminal, before building for the web
  ```

  *(see also the [Emscripten installation instructions](https://emscripten.org/docs/getting_started/downloads.html))*
- **Python 3**, to run a local web server.
  *Note: a browser refuses to load `project.wasm` from a page opened directly as a file.*
- For the examples 3 to 6: the [CGP](https://github.com/drohmer/cgp-2027) library, a C++ library made for
  teaching computer graphics.

## Download the code

Each example page provides a zip of the example and of what it needs. You can also download everything at
once: [interactive-3d-slides.zip](../../code/interactive-3d-slides.zip) (the bridge, the six examples with
their web builds, and the tests). Every file can also be opened individually: [all the files](../../02_more/01_files/index.html).

The web builds are already included in the zips (`examples/<example>/web/`), so that you can show a demo before
compiling anything.

- **&gt;** Unzip the archive, and run a web server from the root of the folder:

  ```
  python3 -m http.server
  ```

- **&gt;** Open `http://localhost:8000/examples/a1-opengl-cube/slides.html` in your browser.
  You should see a slide with a rotating cube.

## A demo in a reveal.js or lhtml-site slide

**reveal.js:** the demo is a frame in a `<section>`. The attribute `data-src` loads it when its slide comes
near, and unloads it after:

```html
<section>
  <iframe class="demo" data-src="web/index.html?speed=0.2"
          style="width: 100%; height: 540px; border: 0"></iframe>
</section>
```

**lhtml-site:** the macro `demo::` writes the frame and sizes it in the layout of the slide:

```
media::
demo::../_demo/cube/index.html?speed=0.2
::
```

- In both cases, the settings of the demo are given in its address (`?speed=0.2`): a single build can
  therefore be used in several slides with different settings.
- The [example talk in reveal.js](../../decks/reveal.js/index.html) and the
  [one in lhtml-site](../../decks/lhtml-site/html/index.html) show the same demos in a complete presentation.

<div class="callout warn"><div class="callout-title">Note: the frame takes the keyboard</div>

Once you click in a demo, the keyboard goes to the frame, and the arrow keys (or the remote control) no longer
change the slide. This is the normal behavior of browsers. To avoid this problem, the page of the demo
(`slide-frame.html`) sends the keys back to the slides, in both tools.

</div>

